a grand parade in the capital nepado  armed forces day is a day of pomp and  circumstance for the myanmar military  but for many others the celebrations  mask a brutal reality  these are images from the northern sagin  region  people here say soldiers from the  myanmar military came to their village  in mid-march and set fire to their homes  one soldier said burnt the house and  another said don't burn it i asked them  where i should go if they burn it and  they told me to sleep underneath a mango  tree i had no time to take anything from  my house my whole body was shaking  the junta has previously denied its  troops committed arson  instead it's accused anti-coup opponents  of starting the fires  in yangon the hustle and bustle of city  life has returned  with a few telltale signs of military  rule  anti-junta resistance may not be obvious  but it's there  most people are afraid to speak publicly  there is no institution that we can't  compare them with they are committing  the lowest and most brutal acts acts  that we can't even imagine  the assistance association for political  prisoners says more than a thousand  seven hundred people have been killed by  security forces since the coup  many protesters have fled to jungle  training camps to take up arms against  the military  that is one big miscalculation that the  junta made yeah they misjudged their own  people  thinking that if you  kill many people all the resistance will  die down it has not happened and so it  shows that they don't understand their  own people and they don't know how to  end this present conflict  in his speech on sunday military leader  minong lang vowed to annihilate those  fighting to overthrow military rule  many political analysts say they fear  the crisis and violence in myanmar is  likely to continue  florence louis al jazeera
 

